The documented differences between the vendor's RM69A10 bring-up and ours
(coproc/test/panel_trace/run.sh applies this to the vendor's register trace
with `patch --fuzz=0` and requires the result to be our trace, byte for byte,
in every scenario of the register model). Trace lines: W addr value (write),
R addr value (read), D microseconds (delay). Hunk by hunk:

Hunk 1, after the display power domain (k230_logo.c 353):
  a. Pads IO22 (0x91105058), IO25 (0x91105064), IO35 (0x9110508c) set to
     GPIO, output and input enabled, Schmitt trigger, drive 15/7/7: the
     values of the board's own U-Boot DT (T-Display-K230_canmv_rt @ abb07090,
     canmv_k230/src/uboot/uboot/arch/riscv/dts/k230_canmv_v3p0.dts 118, 124,
     136). The vendor path relies on U-Boot having applied them.
  b. IO35 (GPIO bank 1 = 0x9140c000, bit 3; net IO35_DISEN, the panel supply
     switch on the schematic) made an output and driven high, then 10 ms
     (the settle time of LilyGO's Linux panel driver after power-on). No
     vendor bring-up drives IO35.
  c. k230_gpio('o', pin, &high) (k230_board_common.c 396-403) writes the
     direction register's old value, with the pin's bit set, into the data
     register: every other output of the bank is driven to its direction
     bit. Ours sets or clears only the pin's bit in the data register's own
     value, so each output step reads the data register (R 0x9140b000) and
     the data register then holds different values in the rest of the
     sequence.

Hunk 2: the VO scaler coefficient tables (display_logo.c 366-383, from
  vo_table.h, which is GPL-2.0) are not written: only video layers 0-2
  scale, and they are never enabled; the framebuffer and the status band are
  OSD layers.

Hunk 3: OSD4 scans the deck framebuffer (PROTOCOL §12) instead of the
  logo: format 2 = RGB565 (Linux drivers/gpu/drm/canaan/canaan_vo.c 198-200,
  linux-xuantie-kernel @ 7d4e1f44) instead of 3 = XRGB8888, address
  COPROC_FB_ADDR + 4096 = 0x08101000 instead of 0x1f000000, stride
  1136 / 8 = 142 instead of 568 * 4 / 8 = 284.

